talia: Quick design sync: proposing an "epoch-based reclaim" layer to handle spillback and partial-page fragmentation without blocking inference. Short thread with API sketch + tradeoffs.

michael: Love the idea. Can you summarize the problem we're solving vs current evictor?

talia: Problem: when we spill to host/nvme and then try to reclaim pages, current per-page refs + immediate free causes races with in-flight inference and lots of metadata churn. We want a model that: 1) avoids stalls on hot-path, 2) makes reclaim idempotent and resumable, 3) reduces thrashing across NVMe <-> GPU.

jing: High-level: epoch ticks + per-request touch-set? How would that avoid races with prefetches?

talia: Sketch: allocator maintains an epoch counter (E). Every inference request gets a local epoch snapshot (e0). Pages accessed during that request get tagged with last_seen_epoch = e0. Reclaim runs advance_epoch(): bump E -> E+1, and only reclaims pages whose last_seen_epoch < E - grace_window. Prefetches/commits that are in-flight include their target epoch in staging; commit only visible if staging_epoch >= current E (or we atomically swap). That yields a global quiesce point without pausing workers.

michael: Pseudo API?

talia: ```
// on request start
int req_epoch = alloc.snapshot_epoch();
// touch a page
alloc.mark_touch(page_id, req_epoch);
// async prefetch returns staging token
Staging s = alloc.prefetch_to_staging(range, /*staging_epoch=*/req_epoch);
// commit -> atomically publish only if staging still valid
alloc.publish_staging(s);
// evictor
alloc.advance_epoch();
reclaimable = alloc.find_reclaimable(E, /*grace=*/2);
alloc.reclaim_pages(reclaimable);
```

jing: Two followups: how to handle tenant isolation (tenant may want stronger retention) and how big is grace window?

talia: Expose per-tenant epoch grace (tenant_config.epoch_grace). Default=2 epochs (~50-100ms depending on epoch tick). Tenant with strict SLOs can bump to 4. Also add an override pin() which pins until next epoch advance for batch workloads.

michael: What about multi-page atomicity? e.g., partial staging published -> inconsistent kv entries?

talia: Staging must publish via a single atomic pointer swap per request-id. Implementation: build a request-staging-list and use a single CAS to swap into the kvcache map. Cancel just abandons staging list (GC by epoch). That avoids partial publish.

jing: GC mechanics? If a staging is abandoned (never published) we need cleanup to avoid leaks.

talia: Simple GC: keep staging linked lists with staging_epoch; background job cleans staging with staging_epoch < E - max_staging_age. Also limit concurrent staging per-worker (configurable) to bound memory.

michael: Any perf numbers or cost model?

talia: Local microbench (synthetic): epoch advance every 25ms, grace=2 -> p99 cold-token down by ~6ms vs synchronous pin. Memory overhead: staging buffers ~0.5-1% of active working set. CPU overhead for epoch bookkeeping <1% in our bench.

jing: Edgecases: what if a page touched late in request after snapshot? We use req_epoch for all touches so late touches still recorded with same epoch. If a long-running request spans many epochs it can bump its snapshot or request a long-lived pin. We should document "long request" pattern.

michael: Sounds good. Observability: we need metrics: epoch_advance_latency, reclaim_batch_size, staging_abandon_rate, per-tenant_epoch_hits.

talia: Agreed — will add telemetry. Also plan: feature gate + canary on low-traffic cluster, enable per-tenant flags.

ops-bot: Created ticket ALLOC-5912: "epoch-based reclaim + spillback staging" and scheduled canary cluster. :gear:

ci-bot: Test harness: https://ci.internal/runs/epoch-reclaim/22 - includes microbench logs and leak check.

michael: Decision call: I support epoch reclaim + staging with conservative defaults (epoch_tick=25ms, grace=2). Implement atomic staging publish and per-tenant overrides. We should postpone automatic per-slab compaction until we observe stable hotsets.

